WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.588
Bitwise operators act on all bit positions of an integer in parallel.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:14.658
Shift right by k and mask with one to read bit k, or shift one left by k to set, clear, or toggle that position.

00:00:14.658 --> 00:00:23.080
Use x.bit_count() and x.bit_length() in modern Python for fast population count and highest-bit lookups.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:29.805
Subtracting one flips the lowest set bit to zero and turns all trailing zeros into ones.

00:00:29.805 --> 00:00:40.486
Therefore x bitwise-and x minus one clears the lowest set bit in a single step, and equals zero for positive x if and only if x is a power of two.

00:00:40.486 --> 00:00:45.607
Meanwhile x bitwise-and negative x isolates that exact lowest set bit.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:54.041
Exclusive-OR is associative, commutative, and its own inverse because every value cancels itself out.

00:00:54.041 --> 00:01:02.102
Prefix XOR arrays answer range XOR queries in O(1) time using the exact same formula as prefix sums.

00:01:02.102 --> 00:01:07.180
Note that addition equals XOR plus twice the bitwise-AND carry.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:16.329
A linear XOR basis compresses a huge array of integers into at most sixty basis vectors indexed by their highest set bit.

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:24.752
To insert x, XOR it with existing basis vectors from top bit down until x becomes zero or claims an empty bit slot.

00:01:24.752 --> 00:01:30.758
Greedily XORing with the basis from highest bit down finds the maximum subset XOR.
